.\" Revision 1.1  2026/10/31 14:32:28  dlr
.\" Initial revision: emailprep(1) man page. Documents the three ordered steps and why the order cannot be rearranged, the four options, the stderr histogram as the way to size a run before launching it, and the distinction between that predicted cost and the waste mdxfind reports as measured at the end of a run. Records the three deliberate non-behaviours -- no lowercasing, no CR stripping, no $HEX[] decoding -- each with the reason, which in every case is that the hash is computed over the bytes as typed. Notes that unlike rling it does accept - for stdin and stdout, since rling creates a file of that name, and that gmail.com and googlemail.com must be run as separate files because their suffixes differ by five bytes and the domain cannot be rewritten to match. States the shared first-@ invariant with mdxfind -E and names the regression target that checks it. Cross-references splitlen(1) as the right tool when the whole line is what is being measured. mandoc -Tlint clean.
.\"
.TH EMAILPREP 1 "2026-11-01" "emailprep 1.6" "Password Recovery"
.SH NAME
emailprep \- normalize, deduplicate and order email addresses by sweep cost
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B emailprep
.RB [ \-d
.IR domain ]...
.RB [ \-N ]
.RB [ \-u ]
.RB [ \-q ]
.I input output
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B emailprep
prepares a list of email addresses for an
.B mdxfind \-E
or
.B mdxfind \-a
run.  It reads
.IR input ,
writes
.IR output ,
and does three things in an order that cannot be rearranged:
.TP 3n
1.
.B Normalize
\(em remove
.B .
from the local part, the text before the first
.BR @ .
Gmail and googlemail ignore dots there, so
.B f.r.esh@gmail.com
and
.B fresh@gmail.com
are one account, and the dotless spelling is the seed that
.B mdxfind \-a
sweeps back out to every dotting.
.TP 3n
2.
.B Deduplicate
\(em which must follow normalization, because that is where the collapse
happens.  In one measured corpus of 10.2 million plaintexts, 27,179 distinct
spellings of a single local part normalize to one seed.  Undeduplicated, each
of those spellings sweeps its own 2^23 candidates.
.TP 3n
3.
.B Order by local-part length,
shortest first \(em which must follow deduplication.  A local part of length
.I L
costs 2^\fIL\fP candidates, so cost per input line spans eight orders of
magnitude inside one unsorted file.  Shortest-first spends the early budget
where candidates-per-find is lowest, which is what matters on a run that is
stopped rather than finished.
.PP
Either filename may name a standard stream instead of a file.  The input may be
written
.B \-
or
.BR stdin ,
and the output
.B \-
or
.BR stdout .
.BR rling (1)
accepts only the words and treats
.B \-
as an ordinary filename, so the set
.B emailprep
accepts is a superset of rling's: a pipeline written in either style reads the
same way through both tools.  A file genuinely named
.B stdin
remains reachable by any path that is not the bare word, such as
.BR ./stdin .
.PP
Ordering is stable: lines of equal local-part length keep their input order, so
two runs over the same input produce byte-identical output.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-d " domain"
Keep only addresses whose domain is
.IR domain .
The comparison ignores case, so
.B @Gmail.com
is kept by
.BR "\-d gmail.com" ,
but the bytes are preserved unchanged \(em the hash is over what was stored.
May be given up to 64 times.  A line with no
.B @
has no domain and is excluded whenever
.B \-d
is given.
.TP
.B \-N
Do not normalize.  Deduplicate and order only, measuring the local part as
written.
.TP
.B \-u
Do not deduplicate.  Normalize and order only.
.TP
.B \-q
Suppress the histogram on standard error.
.SH HISTOGRAM
Unless
.B \-q
is given,
.B emailprep
reports its dispositions and then one row per local-part length with the
.RI 2^ len
sweep that length implies, and the total:
.PP
.RS
.nf
  local len      lines        2^len sweep
          2          1                  4
         14          1           1.64e+04
         30          1           1.07e+09
  total sweep at full width: 1.07e+09 candidates
.fi
.RE
.PP
This is the cost of the run
.I before
it is launched.
.B mdxfind
reports the width it actually used per word at the end of a run, which is the
waste that was
.IR paid ;
the two are different numbers and neither substitutes for the other.
.SH WHAT IT DOES NOT DO
.B emailprep
does not lowercase its input.  That is right for a frequency table and wrong for
a candidate producer: the hash is computed over the bytes as typed.  Rule
.B l
covers it when lowercasing is wanted.
.PP
It
.I does
strip a trailing carriage return, matching
.BR "rehex \-u"
and
.BR rling (1).
The count is reported so a CRLF input is still visible.
.PP
It is not a replacement for
.BR rling (1).
Set subtraction, intersection and the
.B \-q
statistics all still belong there.
.B emailprep
does the one job
.B rling
cannot express \(em ordering by a key that is not the whole line \(em and
nothing else.
.SH $HEX[] ENCODING
.B $HEX[]
input is decoded natively, as in every other tool of the suite, and the decode
happens
.I first
\(em so the
.B @
scan, the
.B \-d
match, normalization, the deduplication key and the length key all operate on
the bytes the address actually consists of.  This is not a convenience: the
wrapper is ASCII and carries no literal
.BR @ ,
so a wrapped address measured as written has no domain at all and buckets at the
width of the wrapper rather than of the local part.
.PP
The decoder matches
.BR "rehex \-u"
exactly: the prefix test is a literal, case-sensitive
.BR $HEX[ ,
and decoding stops at the first byte pair that is not hexadecimal, which is the
closing
.BR ] .
.PP
A wrapped address therefore deduplicates against its raw twin \(em
.B $HEX[]
of
.B j.o.h.n@gmail.com
and a plain
.B john@gmail.com
are one record.
.PP
On output the representation is preserved and only the content changes: a line
that arrived wrapped leaves wrapped, re-encoded from the normalized bytes, and a
line that arrived raw leaves raw.  The single override is a safety rule rather
than a stylistic one \(em a decoded payload can hold a NUL or a newline, which
no raw line can carry, so such a record is always emitted wrapped.
.SH SHARED INVARIANT
.B emailprep
splits on the
.B first
.BR @ ,
exactly as
.B mdxfind \-E
does.  If the two ever disagree, the ordering this tool produces is no longer
the ordering
.BR mdxfind 's
sweep cost follows, and running it stops buying anything.
.B make regress-email
checks the two against one fixture.
.SH EXAMPLES
Prepare the gmail portion of a leak and run the dot sweep against it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ emailprep \-d gmail.com leak.txt gmail\-ordered.txt
$ mdxfind \-a \-m 0 \-f hashes.txt gmail\-ordered.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run
.B gmail.com
and
.B googlemail.com
as separate files.  Their suffixes differ by five bytes, and the domain cannot
be rewritten to match because the hash is over the stored bytes:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ emailprep \-d gmail.com       leak.txt g1.txt
$ emailprep \-d googlemail.com  leak.txt g2.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Read several leaks from a pipe, naming the input the way
.BR rling (1)
does:
.PP
.RS 7n
.nf
$ cat leak*.txt | emailprep \-d gmail.com stdin ordered.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Subtract a wordlist already tried, after preparing:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ emailprep \-d gmail.com leak.txt ordered.txt
$ rling ordered.txt todo.txt already\-tried.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Size a run without writing the list:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ emailprep \-d gmail.com leak.txt /dev/null
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on a usage error, an unreadable input, or an allocation
failure.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mdxfind (1),
.BR rling (1),
.BR splitlen (1),
.BR procrule (1),
.BR rehex (1)
.PP
.BR splitlen (1)
also orders by length, but by
.I line
length and without normalization or deduplication; it is the right tool when
the whole line is the thing being measured.
.SH AUTHOR
Waffle.
